---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import Used from "../../components/Used.astro";
import { stableIconNames } from "@skryensya/core/icon";
import { allSets, iconMarkup } from "../../icons";

/* The roles shown side by side. A handful is enough to see that the drawing changes and the name
 * not; all 32 are up. */
const compared = ["search", "delete", "settings", "user", "warning", "external-link"] as const;

const vocabHtml = `<div
  style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill, minmax(6rem, 1fr)); gap:var(--space-stack-md)"
>
${stableIconNames
  .map(
    (name) => `  <span
    style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; gap:var(--space-stack-xs); min-inline-size:0"
  >
    <span data-sk-icon="${name}" data-sk-icon-size="lg"></span>
    <code style="font-size: var(--font-size-caption)">${name}</code>
  </span>`,
  )
  .join("\n")}
</div>`;

const bind = `# nothing: Phosphor is the @skryensya/react default
# another set? install it, and only then is it bundled:
pnpm add @skryensya/icons-lucide     # or @skryensya/icons-material`;

const swap = `- import { lucideIcons } from "@skryensya/icons-lucide";
+ import { phosphorIcons } from "@skryensya/icons-phosphor";

- <IconSetProvider set={lucideIcons}>
+ <IconSetProvider set={phosphorIcons}>

  // and nothing more. Not a <Icon name="…"> moves.`;
---

<Base
  title="Iconography"
  description="The stable vocabulary of roles, sets as a brand, and why the SVG pattern does not paint fill or stroke."
>
  <h1>Iconography</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The system names <strong>roles</strong>, not drawings. A set (Lucide, Phosphor, Material) is a brand: it chooses the geometry that each role occupies, just as a brand chooses the hue of <code>--ramp-accent-600</code>. How you mount the SVG in React or vanilla is in <a href="/en/components/icon">Icon</a>.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={vocabHtml} label="Vocabulario estable" />
  <p>
    Change brand, mode or contrast above: the icons follow, and do not declare a single color token. They inherit <code>currentColor</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>A pattern, not an extra</h2>
  <p>
    Like the <a href="/en/state-layer">state layer</a>, the pattern comes in <code>tokens.scss</code>. An icon is not an add-on that a component opts into: just something that displays a chevron, a close, or a state glyph needs this same box. The rules are inert until an element has <code>sk-icon</code>, so they do not matter on a page without icons. The decision is in <a href="/decisiones/0015-el-icono-es-un-pattern-y-el-set-es-una-marca">ADR-15</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The stable vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    Thirty-two roles, which cover the most used cases of an app and not just what the system components consume. The reason is portability: if the vocabulary went up to <code>chevron-down</code> and nothing more, each project would declare <code>search</code> or <code>delete</code> on its own, and the most common system role would be called differently in each app.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each name is a <strong>role</strong>, never a drawing: <code>delete</code> and not <code>trash</code>, <code>edit</code> and not <code>pencil</code>, <code>search</code> and not <code>magnifying-glass</code>, <code>visibility</code> and not <code>eye</code>. The name has to remain true when another set draws the different role. And it is <code>danger</code>, not <code>error</code>, because the system already says <code>danger</code> in and in <code>BadgeTone</code>, a word does not coexist with its synonym.
  </p>
  <p>
    The limit did not move: a concept of <strong>product</strong> (<code>invoice</code>, <code>warehouse</code>, <code>airplane-tilt</code>) does not fit, no matter how much it is used in your app. It comes out as your geometry. And each name here is an obligation for whoever draws a set, because a set is complete.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose a set</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>icon set is a</strong> brand. <code>@skryensya/core</code> names the position (<code>chevron-down</code>) and the set says which drawing occupies it. <strong>Phosphor is the default</strong> (travels with React); Lucide and Material are interchangeable, opt-in:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
  <table class="sk-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Paquete</th><th>Bookshop</th><th>Trazo</th><th>viewBox</th><th>Licencia</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>@skryensya/icons-lucide</code></td><td>Lucide</td><td>contorno, 2px</td><td><code>0 0 24 24</code></td><td>ISC</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>@skryensya/icons-phosphor</code></td><td>Phosphor, peso regular</td><td>relleno</td><td><code>0 0 256 256</code></td><td>MIT</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>@skryensya/icons-material</code></td><td>Material Symbols, outlined 400</td><td>relleno</td><td><code>0 -960 960 960</code></td><td>Apache-2.0</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    None live in <code>@skryensya/core</code>: core does not name tenants, and their <code>dependencies</code> are empty. They are separate packages.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={bind} label="install" note="Default Phosphor; the others, opt-in" />

  <h2>The same name, three drawings</h2>
  <p>
    Each row is <strong>a</strong> role; each column, one set. The preview frame has a single set; This table lives on the page to compare all three at once. The name you write is the one on the left, <code>search</code>, not <code>magnifying-glass</code> or the codepoint of a source.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>rol</th>
          {allSets.map((s) => <th>{s.label}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          compared.map((role) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{role}</code></td>
              {allSets.map((s) => (
                <td><Fragment set:html={iconMarkup(role, { size: "lg", set: s.set })} /></td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Three libraries, three different viewBoxes, zero color tokens: all three inherit <code>currentColor</code>.
  </p>
  <p>Changing sets is this, and nothing more:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={swap} label="change set" note="no call site moves" />

  <h2>The CSS does not touch <code>fill</code> or <code>stroke</code></h2>
  <p>
    An outline set draws with <code>fill="none" stroke="currentColor"</code> at the root <code>&lt; svg&gt; </code>, and a <strong>CSS declaration beats a</strong> presentation attribute. A <code>fill: currentColor</code> in the pattern would make every contour set solid, silently. So the intention of the geometry lives in <code>attrs</code>, of the set, and the pattern only puts box and size.
  </p>
  <p>
    That leaves the <strong>icon without its own color</strong>: it inherits <code>currentColor</code>, is correct in all eight brands and the two modes without a token, does not need override on <code>modes/hc.scss</code>, does not enter <code>contrast-pairs.json</code>, and in forced contrast the browser forces the <code>color</code> from the container and <code>currentColor</code> follows it alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Use the pattern</h2>
  <p>
    React (<code>&lt; Icon&gt; </code>), vanilla (<code>mountIcons</code>), sizes and accessibility in the call site: <a href="/en/components/icon">Icon</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
